Create a .pidly tree in a project that has none: the directory itself, a
seeded schema.md documenting the four structural header lines and shipping
the handoff field, .pidly/README.md and the AGENTS.md summary via the same
code path as `pidly agents`, and ignore rules for transient locks and the
.id fallback.

Re-running is a refresh, not a reset: everything generated is rewritten,
schema.md is left alone the moment it exists because from then on the
project owns it. That makes init safe after an upgrade and safe in a hook.

Ignore rules went in .pidly/.gitignore rather than the repo root, so the
tracker is self-contained. Migrating this repo to that layout found its root
rules had been wrong for a while: they read .pidly/**/.lock/ with a trailing
slash, matching only directories, and were never updated when the lock became
a file. git check-ignore confirmed a lock file was not ignored. Nothing was
ever committed, since a lock is removed before its command returns, but the
safety net was not there.

A tracker inside a tracker is refused — the outer one's traversals would walk
the inner one and numbers would collide.

Done: implemented in bin/pidly, covered by tests/init.sh (21 cases).
